# Everything in deploy/ is installed on a Linux box and parsed by something
# that treats a carriage return as content: systemd, nginx, named, logrotate,
# and sh itself. A CRLF checkout on Windows therefore ships a file that the
# target silently refuses rather than reports.
#
# Measured 2026-09-11: /etc/logrotate.d/ezbizi had 67 CR lines and had NEVER
# rotated -- no entry in /var/lib/logrotate/status, zero .gz, a 108MB
# laravel.log against a 50M maxsize. logrotate stopped at line 37, a bare ^M,
# and being unparseable it was skipped in silence. The committed file was
# clean; the copy somebody ran sudo cp from was not.
#
# This was two lines covering *.sh and post-receive, which is the subset that
# happened to have been noticed. The rest of the directory had the same hazard
# and no rule, so the scope is now the whole directory rather than a list that
# grows one incident at a time.
# Every .sh in this repo is run by bash on macOS or Linux -- the release
# scripts on the relay Mac, the deploy scripts on the box. A CRLF shell
# script does not misbehave subtly, it dies at the shebang with
# "bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M". Added 11 Sep 2026 on staging a new
# scripts/mac/ file and watching git announce it was about to do exactly
# that: deploy/ had just been covered and scripts/ had the same hazard and
# no rule, which is the per-directory list this is meant to stop being.
#
# .ps1 is deliberately NOT here. PowerShell is Windows-side and CRLF is
# correct for it.
*.sh text eol=lf

deploy/** text eol=lf
